# Cursor Rules -- Meridian API (Django REST Framework)

Read CLAUDE.md before starting any task. It contains project architecture,
commands, and conventions that must be followed.

## Project Structure

```
meridian/              -- Django project settings
  settings/
    base.py            -- Shared settings
    development.py     -- Dev overrides
    production.py      -- Production settings
apps/
  accounts/            -- Users, auth, profiles
  organizations/       -- Orgs, membership, invitations
  projects/            -- Projects, permissions
  analytics/           -- Data ingestion, aggregation
  notifications/       -- Email, webhook, in-app
  core/                -- Base classes, mixins, utilities
```

## General Rules

- Python 3.12. Use modern syntax: `match` statements, type hints with `|` union, f-strings.
- Type hints on all function signatures. Use `from __future__ import annotations` at the top of every file.
- No wildcard imports (`from module import *`). Always import explicitly.
- Keep files under 300 lines. Split into modules when approaching the limit.
- Docstrings on all public classes and functions (Google style).
- Use `pathlib.Path` instead of `os.path` for file system operations.

## Django Conventions

- Custom user model: `apps.accounts.models.User`. Never import `django.contrib.auth.models.User`.
- All apps registered under `apps/` directory with dotted path: `"apps.accounts"`.
- Settings split by environment. Use `DJANGO_SETTINGS_MODULE=meridian.settings.development`.
- Use `django.conf.settings` for accessing settings, never import settings modules directly.
- URL patterns: app-level `urls.py` with DRF routers, included in root `meridian/urls.py`.

## Model Conventions

- Models inherit from `apps.core.models.BaseModel` which provides `id` (UUID), `created_at`, `updated_at`.
- Use `verbose_name` and `verbose_name_plural` in `Meta`.
- Define `__str__` on every model.
- Related names: explicit and descriptive (`related_name="projects"`, not `related_name="+"`).
- Indexes: define in `Meta.indexes` for fields used in filters and ordering.
- Choices: use `TextChoices` or `IntegerChoices` classes, not tuples.

```python
class Project(BaseModel):
    title = models.CharField(max_length=200)
    status = models.CharField(max_length=20, choices=ProjectStatus.choices, default=ProjectStatus.DRAFT)
    org = models.ForeignKey("organizations.Organization", on_delete=models.CASCADE, related_name="projects")
```

## View Conventions (DRF)

- Use `ModelViewSet` for full CRUD resources. Use `GenericAPIView` for custom endpoints.
- One ViewSet per file in `views/` directory.
- Always define `permission_classes`, `serializer_class`, and `filterset_class`.
- Override `get_queryset()` for org-scoping, never `queryset` class attribute.
- Business logic lives in `services.py`, not in views or serializers.

```python
class ProjectViewSet(OrgScopedMixin, ModelViewSet):
    serializer_class = ProjectSerializer
    permission_classes = [IsAuthenticated, IsOrgMember]
    filterset_class = ProjectFilter

    def get_queryset(self):
        return super().get_queryset().select_related("org").prefetch_related("members")
```

## Serializer Conventions

- One file per resource in `serializers/` directory.
- Use `ModelSerializer` for standard CRUD. Use `Serializer` for custom input/output.
- Validation logic in serializer `validate_<field>()` or `validate()` methods.
- Read-only computed fields: use `SerializerMethodField` with type annotations.
- Nested serializers: read-only by default. Use separate write serializers for nested writes.

## Service Layer

- Business logic in `services.py` as module-level functions or service classes.
- Services accept model instances and primitives, not request objects or serializer data.
- Services handle: validation rules spanning multiple models, side effects, external API calls.
- Services raise domain exceptions from `apps.core.exceptions`.

```python
def create_project(*, org: Organization, created_by: User, title: str, **kwargs) -> Project:
    if Project.objects.filter(org=org, title=title).exists():
        raise DuplicateProjectError(f"Project '{title}' already exists in this org")
    return Project.objects.create(org=org, created_by=created_by, title=title, **kwargs)
```

## Multi-Tenancy

- Org-scoping enforced at the queryset level via `OrgScopedMixin`.
- `OrgScopedMixin.get_queryset()` filters by `self.request.user.org`.
- `OrgScopedMixin.perform_create()` sets `org=self.request.user.org`.
- Manager method `for_org(org)` as the standard scoping entry point.
- Never use `.objects.all()` in views. Always scope to org.
- Admin (`is_staff`) bypasses org-scoping for support tooling.

## Query Optimization

- Always use `select_related()` for ForeignKey fields accessed in serializers.
- Always use `prefetch_related()` for reverse ForeignKey and M2M relationships.
- Use `only()` or `defer()` to limit fields on large tables.
- Add database indexes for fields used in `filter()`, `order_by()`, and `distinct()`.
- Use `django-debug-toolbar` in development to catch N+1 queries.
- Annotate aggregations at the queryset level, not in Python loops.

## Testing

- Use `pytest` with `pytest-django`. Do not use `django.test.TestCase`.
- Factories with `factory-boy` in `tests/factories.py` per app.
- API tests use `rest_framework.test.APIClient`.
- Always test: happy path, validation errors, permission denied, not found.
- Fixtures: use `@pytest.fixture` with factory functions, not Django fixtures.
- Mock external services with `unittest.mock.patch` or `responses` library.
- Mark slow tests with `@pytest.mark.slow`. CI runs slow tests only on `develop`.

```python
@pytest.mark.django_db
class TestProjectAPI:
    def test_create_project(self, auth_client, org):
        response = auth_client.post("/api/v1/projects/", {"title": "New Project"})
        assert response.status_code == 201
        assert response.data["title"] == "New Project"
        assert Project.objects.filter(org=org, title="New Project").exists()

    def test_create_project_unauthenticated(self, client):
        response = client.post("/api/v1/projects/", {"title": "New Project"})
        assert response.status_code == 401

    def test_cannot_see_other_org_projects(self, auth_client, other_org_project):
        response = auth_client.get(f"/api/v1/projects/{other_org_project.id}/")
        assert response.status_code == 404
```

## Celery Tasks

- Tasks in `tasks.py` per app.
- Use `@shared_task` decorator with `bind=True` for retry support.
- Set `autoretry_for`, `retry_backoff`, and `max_retries` on tasks.
- Tasks accept primitive arguments (IDs, strings), not model instances.
- Idempotency: tasks must handle being called multiple times safely.

## Migrations

- Auto-generated by `makemigrations`. Review before committing.
- Never edit a migration that has been merged to `develop`.
- Data migrations in separate files with descriptive names.
- Always test migrations forward and backward: `migrate` and `migrate <app> <previous>`.

## Git Workflow

- Branch from `develop`. Never commit to `main` or `develop` directly.
- Branch naming: `feat/API-123-short-description` or `fix/API-456-short-description`.
- Conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `chore:`, `test:`, `docs:`.
- PR titles: `type(scope): description (API-123)`.
- Keep PRs focused. One logical change per PR. Under 400 lines of diff when possible.

## Security

- Never log or expose secrets, passwords, or tokens.
- Use `django.conf.settings` for sensitive values, loaded from environment variables.
- All input validated through serializers before reaching business logic.
- SQL injection prevented by ORM (never use raw SQL without parameterization).
- CORS configured per-environment in settings.
- Rate limiting via `django-ratelimit` on authentication endpoints.

## Error Handling

- DRF custom exception handler in `apps/core/exceptions.py`.
- Response format: `{ "detail": "message", "code": "ERROR_CODE" }`.
- Custom exceptions inherit from `APIException` with appropriate `status_code`.
- Structured logging with `structlog`. Include request ID for tracing.
- Unhandled exceptions return 500 with generic message (no stack trace in production).
